<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Urban II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1035–<a href="/years/1099">1099</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Urban II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Urban II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 1088 to 1099. In 1095, responding to an appeal from the Byzantine emperor Alexios I framed around the Seljuk threat, he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> that followed were partly a consequence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>'s advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Urban II" width="308" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urbano_II_in_consacrazione_de_l%27altare_del_monastero_di_Cluny_(detail)_(crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Toulouse itself was dominated by the great Romanesque basilica of Saint-Sernin, whose altar <b>Pope Urban II</b> consecrated in 1096.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Jerusalem was created by the First Crusade, launched after <b>Pope Urban II</b>’s appeal at Clermont in 1095.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Manzikert opened Asia Minor to Turkmen settlement and in time helped precipitate the Crusades, as Emperor Alexios I's appeal to <b>Pope Urban II</b> in 1095 was framed around the Turkish threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Urban II</b> elevated the Diocese of Pisa, founded in the 4th century, to the dignity of a metropolitan archdiocese, with its seat in the cathedral of the Assumption.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Raniero Raineri di Bleda, a monk of Cluny, was elected pope as Paschal II after the death of <b>Pope Urban II</b> the previous month.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Elected at Terracina while a rival pope held Rome, <b>Urban II</b> continued Gregory VII's reforms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1076" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Pope Urban II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1076">What was here in 1076? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let those who for a long time, have been robbers, now become knights. Let those who have been fighting against their brothers and relatives now fight in a proper way against the barbarians.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Fulcher of Chartres' version) &#183; tr. Oliver J. Thatcher and Edgar H. McNeal, 1905 &#183; the speech as Fulcher of Chartres, who was there, recorded it &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When an armed attack is made upon the enemy, let this one cry be raised by all the soldiers of God: It is the will of God! It is the will of God!&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Robert the Monk's version) &#183; tr. Dana C. Munro, 1895 &#183; the speech as Robert the Monk, who was there, retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Oh, most valiant soldiers and descendants of invincible ancestors, be not degenerate, but recall the valor of your progenitors.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Robert the Monk's version) &#183; tr. Dana C. Munro, 1895 &#183; the speech as Robert the Monk, who was there, retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You, girt about with the badge of knighthood, are arrogant with great pride; you rage against your brothers and cut each other in pieces. This is not the (true) soldiery of Christ which rends asunder the sheepfold of the Redeemer.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Balderic of Dol's version) &#183; tr. August C. Krey, 1921 &#183; the speech as Balderic of Dol retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Short is the way, little the labor, which, nevertheless, will repay you with the crown that fadeth not away.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Balderic of Dol's version) &#183; tr. August C. Krey, 1921 &#183; the speech as Balderic of Dol retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
